function PostSSNMismatch() {
  return (<article className="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-14"><Meta series="Scenario Deep Dive" color="#1B4332" date="July 28, 2026" time="3 min read" /><h1 className="mb-6 text-[32px] font-bold leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.02em] md:text-[40px]" style={{ color: BRAND.nearblack }}>SSN Mismatch on a Mortgage File: Fraud or Typo?</h1><div className="prose prose-slate max-w-none"><h2>Identity Flags Stop Files. Documentation Resolves Them.</h2><p>An identity verification failure is not a verdict. It is a condition. The fraud screening system flagged something it could not confirm. What happens next depends entirely on what the flag actually represents.</p><h2>Why Identity Flags Appear</h2><p>Most identity flags are not fraud. They fall into four categories: data entry errors (transposed digit in an SSN), name change conflicts (credit file entries under multiple names), verification system limitations (limited credit history or recent immigration), and occasionally actual identity misrepresentation.</p><h2>Patricia Okafor (APP-CL-M14): Super-Prime Borrower, Incomplete Verification</h2><p>Patricia Okafor is a Deloitte Consulting employee with seven years of tenure. FICO 762. Zero derogatory marks. W2 income of $125,000 verified at 97% confidence. DTI 39.89%. Identity verification returned: SSN unverified. Government-issued ID unverified. Fraud score 0.68. Nothing in the credit profile suggests identity misrepresentation. The next step documented in the AI memo is direct: complete SSN and DL verification immediately.</p><h2>How to Work the File</h2><p>Request SSN card and government-issued photo ID directly from the borrower. Compare the SSN against the credit file, URLA, and identity data in the file. If they match and there are no inconsistencies, document the review and clear the condition. If the documents create new questions, the file needs to go to the BSA officer.</p><h2>What the Fraud Score Is and Is Not</h2><p>A fraud score of 0.68 does not mean there is a 68% probability of fraud. It means the aggregate of available signals produced a score in a range that requires human review before the file can advance. In this file, the memo points to document verification as the primary gap.</p><h2>See It in Accord</h2><p>The AI memo for APP-CL-M14 identifies the verification gap, the fraud score, and the specific next step before the underwriter opens the first document.</p></div><Img src="/blog/screenshots/patricia-okafor-ssn-flag.png" alt="Patricia Okafor — SSN unverified, fraud score 0.68" caption="APP-CL-M14 — FICO 762, $125K Deloitte W2 verified at 97%, SSN and DL unverified, fraud score 0.68, BSA/AML requirements cited" /><BL><p>Identity flags require resolution, not reaction. Most are documentation gaps. Some require BSA review. None should be resolved by ignoring the flag and approving the file anyway.</p></BL><CTA /><Back /></article>)
}

function PostEmploymentDateConflict() {
  return (<article className="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-14"><Meta series="Scenario Deep Dive" color="#1B4332" date="July 28, 2026" time="3 min read" /><h1 className="mb-6 text-[32px] font-bold leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.02em] md:text-[40px]" style={{ color: BRAND.nearblack }}>When AUS Has Not Been Run: The Pre-Conditions Every Mortgage File Needs Before Underwriting</h1><div className="prose prose-slate max-w-none"><h2>A File Can Look Perfect and Still Be Ununderwritable</h2><p>Not every underwriting delay is caused by credit, income, or collateral. Sometimes the borrower qualifies. The documentation is complete. The debt-to-income ratio works. But the file still cannot move. Because underwriting has prerequisites. If they are not satisfied before the file reaches an underwriter, no credit decision can be made — regardless of how strong the borrower looks.</p><h2>Diana Foster: A File That Was Not Ready</h2><p>Diana Foster (APP-CL-M10) appears straightforward. FICO 735. Verified W2 income $105,000 at 97% confidence. Stable employment, 7 years. No income discrepancies. Qualifying credit profile. On paper, this looks like a routine approval. Instead, the AI review identified five issues preventing the file from advancing: AUS not submitted. Interest rate not locked. SSN not verified. Government-issued ID not verified. Zero documented post-closing reserves on a 91.76% LTV loan, triggering a layered-risk review. None of these issues suggest Diana is an unqualified borrower. They indicate the file reached underwriting before processing was complete.</p><h2>The Difference Between Processing and Underwriting</h2><p>Processing prepares a loan for underwriting. Underwriting evaluates whether the loan qualifies. Those are different responsibilities. When required preconditions are missing, underwriters stop evaluating risk and start chasing documents — a workflow that adds no credit value while extending turn times. Files bounce back to processing, underwriting queues grow, borrowers wait longer, lock periods shorten, closing dates become harder to meet.</p><h2>Four Missing Pre-Conditions That Stop a File</h2><p><strong>AUS Has Not Been Run.</strong> Without an AUS finding, agency eligibility has not been established, underwriting conditions are unknown, and manual underwriting may be required. The file cannot proceed as though it already has an Approve/Eligible recommendation.</p><p><strong>The Interest Rate Is Not Locked.</strong> Without a locked rate, principal and interest payments may change, qualifying ratios remain estimates, and product eligibility can shift. Until the payment is finalized, every DTI calculation is provisional.</p><p><strong>Identity Has Not Been Verified.</strong> Credit quality does not matter if identity cannot be confirmed. An unresolved SSN or government ID verification blocks the file from closing and may require fraud or BSA review.</p><p><strong>Product Eligibility Has Not Been Confirmed.</strong> Even qualified borrowers can become ineligible if the selected loan program does not match the property characteristics, occupancy, loan amount, or LTV. Before underwriting begins, processing should confirm that the loan fits the selected product.</p><h2>See It in Accord</h2><p>The AI memo for APP-CL-M10 identifies every prerequisite that remains incomplete before an underwriter reviews the file — with the specific next step for each issue.</p></div><Img src="/blog/screenshots/diana-foster-pre-conditions.png" alt="Diana Foster — APP-CL-M10 pre-conditions block" caption="APP-CL-M10 — FICO 735, $105K W2 verified at 97%, but AUS not run, rate not locked, SSN unverified, zero reserves" /><BL><p>Strong borrowers do not always produce underwritable files. Missing prerequisites — not weak credit — are often what prevent underwriting from starting. When lenders identify those issues during processing instead of during underwriting, files move through the pipeline faster, underwriters spend more time making credit decisions, and borrowers reach closing with fewer avoidable delays.</p></BL><CTA /><Back /></article>)
}
function PostAppraisalScanUnreadable() {
  return (<article className="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-14"><Meta series="Scenario Deep Dive" color="#1B4332" date="July 28, 2026" time="3 min read" /><h1 className="mb-6 text-[32px] font-bold leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.02em] md:text-[40px]" style={{ color: BRAND.nearblack }}>FHA vs. Conventional: The Program Mismatch That Delays Underwriting</h1><div className="prose prose-slate max-w-none"><h2>A File Can Have the Right Borrower and the Wrong Loan Program</h2><p>A program mismatch occurs when the loan type documented in the application does not match the loan program selected in the LOS. The URLA identifies the loan as FHA while the file is configured as a Conventional 30-Year Fixed loan. Until that discrepancy is resolved, the file cannot be evaluated using a single set of underwriting guidelines. Mortgage insurance, eligibility requirements, pricing, AUS findings, and investor rules all depend on the selected program. If the program is wrong, every downstream calculation becomes questionable.</p><h2>Why Program Selection Matters</h2><p>FHA and conventional loans follow different underwriting standards. Changing the loan program can affect minimum credit score requirements, maximum allowable DTI, loan-to-value eligibility, mortgage insurance structure, pricing and rate options, AUS submission path, and investor eligibility. Because these requirements differ, underwriters cannot validate eligibility until the program itself is confirmed.</p><h2>Marcus Delgado: A Real Processing Conflict</h2><p>Marcus Delgado (APP-CL-D06) is purchasing a home for $268,000 with a 658 FICO score. The file received a conditional approval, but one issue prevented final underwriting: URLA loan type FHA, selected program Conventional 30-Year Fixed. The AI review identified the inconsistency immediately. Until the loan program is corrected, key underwriting inputs — including mortgage insurance, pricing, AUS findings, and eligibility rules — cannot be validated against a consistent guideline set. The issue is not Marcus qualifications. It is that the file is attempting to satisfy two different underwriting frameworks at the same time.</p><h2>Why These Conflicts Happen</h2><p>Program mismatches usually originate during processing. Common causes: a loan officer initially selected the wrong product; the borrower switched from FHA to conventional but the application was not updated; the LOS auto-populated conflicting fields after a product change. These issues often remain hidden until the file reaches underwriting, where they immediately become blocking conditions.</p><h2>Resolve the Program Before Evaluating the File</h2><p>When a program conflict is identified: confirm the intended loan product with the loan officer; update the URLA and LOS so both reflect the same program; re-run AUS using the correct loan type; recalculate pricing, mortgage insurance, and qualifying ratios; continue underwriting under one consistent guideline. Resolving the conflict early prevents unnecessary underwriting work.</p><h2>See It in Accord</h2><p>The AI underwriting memo for APP-CL-D06 flags the program mismatch before an underwriter begins reviewing documents — FHA loan type on URLA, Conventional 30-Year Fixed program selected, eligibility cannot be confirmed until program alignment is complete.</p></div><Img src="/blog/screenshots/marcus-delgado-program-conflict.png" alt="Marcus Delgado — FHA vs Conventional program conflict" caption="APP-CL-D06 — FICO 658, $268K, FHA loan type vs Conv 30yr Fixed program, eligibility indeterminate" /><BL><p>A program mismatch is not a credit issue — it is a data consistency issue. When the application and loan program point to different underwriting paths, every downstream calculation becomes unreliable. Identifying the conflict before detailed underwriting begins keeps the file moving and ensures eligibility is evaluated under the correct set of guidelines the first time.</p></BL><CTA /><Back /></article>)
}
function PostARMDTITrap() {
  return (<article className="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-14"><Meta series="Scenario Deep Dive" color="#1B4332" date="July 28, 2026" time="3 min read" /><h1 className="mb-6 text-[32px] font-bold leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.02em] md:text-[40px]" style={{ color: BRAND.nearblack }}>The ARM DTI Trap: Why the Teaser Rate Does Not Determine Eligibility</h1><div className="prose prose-slate max-w-none"><h2>The Payment Borrowers See Is Not Always the Payment Underwriters Use</h2><p>Adjustable-rate mortgages often begin with an attractive introductory interest rate. But that is not necessarily the rate used to determine whether the borrower qualifies. For many ARM products, underwriting guidelines require qualification using the fully indexed rate or another qualifying rate specified by the applicable agency or investor guidelines. If the DTI calculation is based only on the teaser rate, the qualifying payment may be understated. A loan that appears to pass on first review can ultimately require additional analysis — or fail eligibility entirely — once the correct qualifying rate is applied.</p><h2>Why the Qualifying Rate Matters</h2><p>A difference of just two percentage points can materially change a monthly mortgage payment. On a 7/1 ARM with a 5.50% introductory rate and a 7.875% fully indexed rate, the difference on a $485,000 mortgage can increase the monthly payment by hundreds of dollars. That higher payment directly affects the qualifying DTI. If the wrong rate is used, every downstream calculation may need to be recalculated.</p><h2>Rachel Torres: One File, Two Different Rates</h2><p>Rachel Torres (APP-CL-ARM02) has a 728 FICO score, $142,000 verified W-2 income, and an initial back-end DTI of 34.8%. At first glance, the file appears well within program guidelines. The AI review identified a critical inconsistency: application shows 5.50%, URLA reflects 7.00%. With conflicting source documents, the qualifying rate cannot be confirmed. Until the correct rate is established, the DTI calculation cannot be relied upon because it is based on an unverified payment.</p><h2>Rate Is Not the Only Requirement</h2><p>Interest rate verification is only one part of ARM underwriting. The file must also document index and margin, periodic adjustment caps, lifetime caps, rate adjustment schedule, and required ARM disclosures. Without the cap structure, the lender cannot accurately evaluate future payment adjustments or confirm required ARM disclosures. These documentation gaps delay underwriting even when the borrower otherwise qualifies.</p><h2>Before an ARM DTI Can Be Trusted</h2><ul><li>Interest rate consistent across application, URLA, and rate lock</li><li>Index and margin documented</li><li>Periodic and lifetime adjustment caps complete</li><li>Required ARM disclosures delivered and documented</li><li>Qualifying DTI calculated using the applicable qualifying rate</li></ul><h2>See It in Accord</h2><p>The AI review for APP-CL-ARM02 identifies the ARM documentation issues before an underwriter opens the file — interest rate conflict between application and URLA, missing cap structure, qualifying payment requires verification, DTI cannot be finalized until the applicable qualifying rate is confirmed.</p></div><Img src="/blog/screenshots/CL-ARM02-DTI-analyst.png" alt="CL-ARM02 DTI Analyst — ARM rate discrepancy" caption="APP-CL-ARM02 — DTI 34.8% at teaser, ARM rate conflict (URLA 7.0% vs Application 5.5%), cap structure absent" /><BL><p>A borrower may qualify at the introductory ARM rate and still require a different qualifying payment under the applicable underwriting guidelines. Verifying the interest rate, documenting the ARM structure, and calculating DTI using the correct qualifying rate ensures the underwriting decision is based on the loan that will actually be delivered — not simply the rate shown on the initial application.</p></BL><CTA /><Back /></article>)
}
function PostCHARMBookletMissing() {
  return (<article className="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-14"><Meta series="Scenario Deep Dive" color="#1B4332" date="July 28, 2026" time="3 min read" /><h1 className="mb-6 text-[32px] font-bold leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.02em] md:text-[40px]" style={{ color: BRAND.nearblack }}>The $1.25M Jumbo ARM That Could Not Close: Title, LTV, and the Documentation That Matters</h1><div className="prose prose-slate max-w-none"><h2>Strong Borrowers Can Still Have Blocked Loans</h2><p>A high credit score does not guarantee a smooth closing. APP-CL-ARM04 is a $1,250,000 jumbo ARM purchase for an Apple employee with a 782 FICO score, verified W-2 income, no derogatory credit history, and a 31.1% back-end DTI. From a credit perspective, it is an exceptionally strong file. Yet the loan cannot close — not because of repayment risk, but because four conditions remain unresolved.</p><h2>Four Issues Are Holding Up the Closing</h2><p><strong>1. Title Has Not Cleared.</strong> The title commitment has been received, but the title work has not been cleared for closing. Until the lender can confirm lien priority and obtain required title insurance coverage, the loan cannot fund. Title conditions frequently surface late in the process, making them some of the most disruptive issues in a closing pipeline. By the time they are discovered, the borrower, lender, and settlement agent are often working toward a scheduled closing date.</p><p><strong>2. The LTV Requires an Exception.</strong> The loan LTV of 92.59% exceeds the lender standard jumbo portfolio guideline. That does not necessarily make the loan ineligible. With a 782 FICO, stable employment, and a strong DTI, the borrower has meaningful compensating factors. But an exception still requires formal documentation and approval under the lender credit policy before underwriting can issue a final approval.</p><p><strong>3. Interest Rate Conflict.</strong> The application shows an interest rate of 6.25%, while the URLA and AUS reflect 7.0%. A Closing Disclosure cannot be issued until the rate is reconciled across all documents. This is not a minor data entry issue — the CD must reflect the actual loan terms, and issuing it with a conflicting rate creates a TRID violation. The TRID deadline for this file is 2026-09-12 for a 2026-09-15 closing.</p><p><strong>4. Identity Verification Incomplete.</strong> Identity match confidence returned 0.0 — the lowest possible score. Fraud screening remains at recommend status with a fraud score of 0.274. Until SSN and government-issued ID are verified and identity is confirmed, the file cannot advance to closing. This condition must be resolved regardless of the strength of the credit profile.</p><h2>Why These Issues Matter</h2><p>None of these conditions change the borrower financial profile. They change whether the lender has sufficient documentation to originate the loan safely and compliantly. Operational conditions — including title clearance, policy exceptions, and required disclosures — often become the final hurdles before funding because they depend on coordination across underwriting, processing, compliance, settlement, and closing. Finding them earlier keeps closing dates intact.</p><h2>See It in Accord</h2><p>Before an underwriter begins reviewing documents, the AI memo for APP-CL-ARM04 identifies each outstanding condition and categorizes it by workflow — title clearance pending, jumbo LTV exception required, interest rate conflict between application and URLA/AUS, identity verification incomplete — explaining exactly what is preventing the loan from moving to clear-to-close and identifying the next action required for each item.</p></div><Img src="/blog/screenshots/arm04-jumbo-blocks.png" alt="APP-CL-ARM04 — jumbo ARM blocks" caption="APP-CL-ARM04 — Apple W2, FICO 782, $1.25M jumbo 10/1 ARM, DTI 31.1%, title defect, LTV exception required, rate conflict 6.25% vs 7.0%, identity unverified" /><BL><p>Not every delayed closing is caused by credit risk. Some of the most expensive delays occur on the strongest borrower files because operational conditions remain unresolved until the end of the process. Identifying title issues, policy exceptions, and disclosure gaps before the loan reaches closing gives lenders time to resolve them without putting funding dates or borrower expectations at risk.</p></BL><CTA /><Back /></article>)
}
